# Lightweight pty/job-control integration coverage for `fs:usage`: confirms
# backgrounding and `terminate` both work through a real interactive session
# without hanging or corrupting it. The actual claim this plan cares about -- that
# `terminate`/`pause` now stop the recursive walk promptly instead of only being
# noticed once it happens to hit an output `send()` -- is proven by the fast,
# deterministic Rust unit test `size_is_interrupted_by_terminate` in
# src/builtins/fs/usage.rs itself (using a synthetic, sleep-delayed in-memory
# `DirectoryLister`): reproducing that scenario through a real pty needs a
# multi-hundred-thousand-file directory tree slow enough to still be scanning after
# `terminate`, and creating/deleting one that large on a real filesystem does not fit
# in this test harness's own 30-second per-test hang-detection budget (confirmed
# directly: a 500,000-file version of this test reliably exceeded it).
#
# A plain `fs:usage .` on this repo's own small tree finishes in well under the time
# it takes to check its status, so the job backgrounded here also runs a `sleep`
# after it, purely to keep the *job* alive long enough to check/terminate it --
# `fs:usage` itself still runs for real as the first statement.
source tests/pty_test_helpers.crush
$t := $(pty_spawn)

$t:run "{fs:usage . ; sleep $(duration:of seconds=100)} &"
pty_assert_job_status $t 0 "Running"
$t:run "terminate 0"
pty_assert_job_status $t 0 "Terminated"

$t:close
echo "ok"
